  "strategy": "After carefully considering the task constraints and evaluation function, I designed an entirely new strategy involving a tighter integration of optimization-based local perturbations and explore-exploit dynamics. The central idea is to use a combination of nonlinear optimization on a 1D prediction function, which has been shown to effectively govern the objective in many cases, with stochastic gradient-based local optimizations.\n\nThe function to minimize is: 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)[k])/ (sum(a))^2, and the sequence must have nonnegative elements. My approach involves several key changes from the previous iteration:\n\n1. Instead of just coordinate descent and LP polishing, I introduce two-phase optimization: first a global stochastic phase using adaptive neighborhoods, and then a phase focusing on very promising time and coordinate positions.\n2. I incorporate a normalized cumulative-sum transformation (conservative impulse placement) to prioritize peak reduction while avoiding simple spike structures that can overfit.\n3. The main optimization uses 1D interpolation of objective response surfaces guided by smooth active constraints, avoiding the full gradient descent computational burden.\n\nDetailed algorithm specification:\n\nvalidate_parent()\n    If global HEIGHT_SEQUENCE_1 is available:\n        parent_arr = np.array(HEIGHT_SEQUENCE_1, dtype=float)\n        if len(parent_arr)>0 and np.all(np.isfinite(parent_arr)):\n            s = np.sum(parent_arr)\n            if s>0.01:\n                normalized_parent = parent_arr/s\n                return normalized_parent, 2*len(parent_arr)*np.max(compute_conv(normalized_parent))\n\n    Else:\n        Generate default candidate with length 256 (mid-range to balance coefficient value and cost)\n        a = np.ones(256)\n        Define fallback optimizations:\n            Optimized candidate must have at least 0.01 positive sum:\n\n    core_strategy = \n\n    Steps per phase:\n        Construct a sequence of length n (default 256)\n        Create invariants based on convolution index performance (allows early pruning of worst-performing coordinates)\n        Define active cost function: min proportion to reduce peaks\n        Use analytical gradients at critical indices (convolutions contribute differentially)\n\n    However, note: the function evaluate_sequence must be called only on least worst candidates to validate whole-gradient movement, a previous approach that relied solely on 1D subproblems lacked complete optimization coverage.\n\n    Finally, we return the best sequence found, with fallback being a normalized sequence at 1000 points if no improvements made.",
